README-DVI -- the eleven DVI drivers, and which one to take===========================================================TeX writes a .dvi: a device-INDEPENDENT description of a page, with no ideawhat a printer is.  A DVI driver turns that into one printer's own language.Eleven of them are in CMDS/TEXCMDS and they are all the same program with adifferent back end, so what separates them is the machine, the resolution,and nothing else.Take the one that matches your printer.  If none does, take `dvitype', whichturns the DVI into readable text and is the only one that needs no printer atall.The table---------Every figure below was measured on this disk on 2026-09-02, by running eachdriver on SYS/TEX/SAMPLES/story.tex and reading what it asked for.  driver    size   writes         printer                         wants  ------    ----   ------         -------                         -----  dvialw   64794   .dvi_alw       Apple LaserWriter (PostScript)  300 dpi  dvidjp   55778   .dvi_djc       HP DeskJet Plus                 300 dpi  dvieps   55532   .dvi_eps       Epson 9-pin, 240x216            240 dpi  dviimp   54676   .dvi_imp       Imagen, imPRESS                 300 dpi  dvijep   56884   .dvi_jep       HP LaserJet Plus                300 dpi  dvijet   54410   .dvi_jet       HP 2686A LaserJet               120 dpi  dvilj2   57128   .dvi_lj2       HP LaserJet II                  300 dpi  dvimac   54526   .dvi_mac       Apple ImageWriter, 144 dpi      144 dpi  dvioki   54970   .dvi_oki       Okidata 192, 144 dpi            144 dpi  dvitos   55070   .dvi_tos       Toshiba P-1351, 180x180         166 dpi  dvips    93342   .ps            PostScript (dvipsk 5.495b)      -- see below  dvitype  73712   standard out   none: text                      noneTwo of those look like typing errors and are not.  **`dvidjp' writes `_djc'**,not `_djp' -- it is the DeskJet driver built from the LaserJet one and itkept the LaserJet's suffix letter.  And **`dvijet' asks for 120 dpi** whereevery other laser driver asks for 300; that is the 2686A, the firstLaserJet, and it is the reason `dvijet' is the cheapest one to demonstrate.None of them takes the output filename as an argument.  Each writes besidethe .dvi, and each writes a log at `<name>.dvi_err'.They will all warn about fonts, and that is fixable---------------------------------------------------    Font file [cmr10 [300 dpi]] could not be opened; 0 font files are open    Proceeding with zero size characters for this fontSYS/TEX/FONTS carries .tfm METRICS and no bitmaps, so out of the box everydriver sets the text at zero size and the page comes out nearly empty.  Thisis not a fault in the drivers and it is not permanent: **Metafont is on thisdisk and renders the font you are missing.**  DOC/README-METAFONT is thewhole account; the short form for `dvijet', whose 120 dpi `epsonlo' matchesexactly, is    export MFINPUTS=.:/dd/SYS/TEX/MFINPUTS/CM:/dd/SYS/TEX/MFINPUTS    virmf '&cmbase' '\scrollmode; \mode:=epsonlo; \input cmr10; \end'    gftopk cmr10.120gf /dd/tmp/cmr10.120pk    export TEXFONTS=/dd/tmp    export FONTLIST=pk    dvijet story.dviand the page goes from 3138 bytes to 7350.  Render every font the documentuses -- the sample wants cmsl10 and cmbx10 as well -- or the ones you skipstay zero-size.  Pick the mode out of MFINPUTS/modes.mf that matches theresolution the driver asked for: CanonCX is 300 dpi, epsonlo 120.A .pk does not ship, and should not: it is made for ONE printer at ONEresolution.  What ships is the means to make it.  SYS/TEX/FONTS has emptyPK300 and PK144 directories waiting for the two somebody expected to want.`dvips' is the exception------------------------It stops before it reaches the font question:    dvips: ! Couldn't find header file tex.pro`tex.pro' is dvips's PostScript prologue and there is no .pro file anywhereon this disk.  Fonts will not help it.  If you want PostScript, `dvialw' isthe other PostScript driver here and it works -- 2687 bytes for the samplewith no fonts at all.`dvitype' needs no printer--------------------------    dvitype story.dvi < /nilprints the DVI as text: the preamble, the fonts the document asks for byname and size, and every positioning command.  It is the one to reach forwhen the question is "what is actually in this file", and it is how theresolutions in the table above were checked.`< /nil' IS NOT OPTIONAL FROM A SCRIPT.  dvitype asks five questions --output level, starting page, maximum pages, device resolution, magnification-- and takes the default for each at end of file.  Without it, run from ascript, it prints nothing at all and waits.